$ 
$ Basis set library file automatically generatered by basis_set.py
$ 
$ invocation: basis_set.py --file=../basis/reference_unedited/5f_qz_archive --print_polarization --print_scfbas --uncontracted --equal_exponent_threshold=0.05 --polarization_type=5f correlating,6d/7s correlating,6s6p correlating,5d correlating,5f polarizing --output=dyall.5f.qz.bas
$ 
$ These are the sets of functions selected from the TCA archive (apart from SCF):
$  ['5f correlating', '6d/7s correlating', '6s6p correlating', '5d correlating', '5f polarizing']
$ 
$ dyall.5f.qz.bas
$
$  REFERENCE
$ K.G. Dyall, Theor. Chem. Acc. (2007) 117:491.
$ Available from the Dirac web site, http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$
$ Elements Supported
$ Md Ac Cm Pu No Am Cf Bk Pa U Th Np Lr Fm Es
$
$ Description
$ 
$ 5f-Block Quadruple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
$ =========================================
$ 
$ 
$ Date: 2 Mar 2007
$ 
$ 
$ When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:
$ 
$ K.G. Dyall, Theor. Chem. Acc. (2007) 117:491.
$ Available from the Dirac web site, http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
$ given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the triple-zeta basis sets described in the above article.  The contractions
$ are taken from SCF calculations on a weighted average of the valence configurations:
$ 
$ * For Ac, Pa, U, Np, Cm, and Lr, where the 6d is occupied in the ground state, the
$ 5f^n-1 6d^1 7s^2, and 5f^n-1 7p^1 7s^2 configurations were used with a 100:6 weight ratio.
$ Note that for Ac the 5f is empty: this element is treated as a transition metal.
$ 
$ * For Th, the 6d^2 7s^2, 5f^1 6d^1 7s^2 and  6d^1 7s^2 7p^1 configurations were used,
$ with a weight ratio of 100:10:6
$ 
$ * For the rest (Pu, Am, Cf - No), the 5f^n 7s^2,5f^n-1 6d^1 7s^2, and 5f^n-1 7p^1 7s^2
$ configurations were used with a weight ratio of 100:10:6
$ 
$ 
$ The archive is set out as follows.
$ 
$ 1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
$    of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small 
$    component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
$    symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
$    those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
$    The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
$    j=l+1/2.
$ 
$ 2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
$    functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
$    followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
$    transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
$    symmetry type. 
$ 
$ 3. Exponents of correlating functions for the 5f shell, the 6d and 7s shells, 
$    and the 5d shell.
$ 
$ 4. Exponents of polarizing functions for the 5f shell and for the 6d and 7s shells.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ Basis set construction.
$ ----------------------
$ 
$ Contracted basis sets can be constructed by adding primitive functions to the SCF 
$ set. These basis sets are described below. Note that the term "quadruple zeta" applies 
$ to the s and p set; the d set is quintuple zeta and the f set sextuple zeta. This is 
$ a result of keeping a reasonable energy balance for the basis sets. 
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the function
$       with the largest exponent.
$ 
$ 
$ 1. Valence flexibility. To the SCF occupied set, add the following:
$ 
$    For 6d/7s correlation
$    1st, 2nd, and 4th s primitives from the SCF set 
$    2nd, 3rd, and 4th p primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 2nd, and 3rd d primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 2nd, and 3rd f primitives from the SCF set
$    1st correlating f function for the 6d and 7s shells, except Ac-U and Pu-Am
$    Correlating 2g1h functions for the 6d and 7s shells.
$ 
$    For 6s/6p/5f correlation
$    5th, 6th, and 8th s primitives from the SCF set 
$    6th, 7th, and 8th p primitives from the SCF set
$    4th, 5th, and 6th d primitives from the SCF set
$    2nd, 3rd, and 4th f primitives from the SCF set
$    Correlating 3g2h1i functions for the 5f shell.
$ 
$    The combined set is therefore:
$    1st, 2nd, 4th, 5th, 6th, and 8th s primitives from the SCF set 
$    2nd, 3rd, 4th, 6th, 7th, and 8th p primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, 5th, and 6th d primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 2nd, 3rd, and 4th f primitives from the SCF set
$    1st correlating f function for the 6d and 7s shells, except Ac-U and Pu-Am
$    Correlating 2g1h functions for the 6d and 7s shells.
$    Correlating 3g2h1i functions for the 5f shell.
$ 
$    This set is likely to display linear dependence in the d set. 
$    If this is the case, and your software does not automatically delete
$    linearly dependent basis functions, delete either the 6d contraction, 
$    or delete one of the first five primitives. 
$ 
$    The 1st correlating f function is close to the outermost SCF function for 
$    several of the actinides; the exponent ratio varies irregularly across the 
$    row. For Bk and Cf the inclusion of this function is marginal. A ratio of 
$    1.4 - 1.5 is desirable in order to avoid linear dependence.
$ 
$    There is also likely to be linear dependence in the g set for Pa - Pu.
$    If this is the case, delete the innermost g from the 6d/7s correlating set.
$ 
$ 2. 5d correlation. To basis set 1, add the following:
$    9th, 10th, and 11th s primitives from the SCF set 
$    9th, 10th, and 11th p primitives from the SCF set
$    7th d primitive from the SCF set
$    5th and 6th f primitives from the SCF set
$    Correlating 2g1h functions for the 5d shell for Ac.
$ 
$    The core correlating 2g1h functions are close enough to the inner 2g1h from the 5f 
$    correlating set that extra functions are not needed for all except Ac.
$ 
$ 3. Dipole polarization of the 5f. Add the following functions:
$    Polarizing g function for Pa - Pu and Lr
$    Polarizing h function for Pa - U and Lr
$    Polarizing i function
$ 
$    The polarizing g and h exponent are close to the correlating g and h exponents
$    for the 6d shell for most of the row. Except for those listed above, adding
$    them is likely to result in linear dependence. 
$ 
$ 4. Dipole polarization of the 6d. Add the polarizing f, g, and h functions.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 

